# rules: go-tmpfile-predictable
# languages: go
# fixture: go/tmpfile-predictable.go
# from: go/lang/security/bad_tmp.yaml (bad-tmp-file-creation)
#
# CWE-377, a file created at a name anyone can predict in a directory anyone
# can write to.
#
# The original writes the path check inline, as `"=~//tmp/.*$/"` - a literal
# argument that is really a regex. pwrq splits the two: the pattern
# says there is an argument, and where_capture says what it has to look like.
# The regex tests the source text of the literal, quotes included, which is why
# it is anchored at a quote rather than at a slash.
#
# The trailing $$$_ is "and whatever else" - the `...` the original writes as
# `ioutil.WriteFile("...", ...)`. Without it the pattern would ask for
# a two-argument WriteFile, because a pattern says how many arguments a call
# has; with it the pattern says only that the first one is the path.
#
# os.WriteFile is here and not in the original, which predates it. A rule that
# only knows the io/ioutil spelling misses every file written since Go 1.16.

["ioutil.WriteFile($P, $$$_)", "os.WriteFile($P, $$$_)", "os.Create($P)"] as $writes
| scan_ast("*.go"; $writes)
| where_capture("P"; "^\"/tmp/")
| finding("go-tmpfile-predictable";
    "$P is a predictable name in a world-writable directory; "
    + "os.CreateTemp picks one nobody can guess")
| report
